00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:10.880
inspect embedding geometry without installing software. Okay, let's pop the hood on token embedding

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:16.780
geometry. Everyone treats token embeddings as a fixed lookup table whose nearest neighbors are

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:23.000
obvious and stable. In practice, the geometry is shaped by the training objective and the projection

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:29.260
method used to inspect it. When coordinates are left continuous, neighbors of Trump include other

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:35.600
presidents and family names because magnitude information is preserved. After simple discretization,

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:40.960
the same token suddenly sits among generic political terms because the thresholding step

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:46.840
discards the very signals that distinguish specific entities. The difference arises because

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:51.800
discretization throws away magnitude information that the original training preserved.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:58.280
This adds no runtime cost but changes which analogies surface in embedding-based retrieval or

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:04.860
clustering pipelines. The practical takeaway is to test both continuous and thresholded views before

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:11.840
deciding which neighbors matter for your downstream task. Run the ASCI term draw hyphen bench

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:18.540
